# Copy to .env and fill in as needed. All have safe local defaults.

PORT=4000

# Postgres. Leave EMPTY locally: the API then runs PGlite (embedded Postgres)
# under api/data/pg — no install needed, same SQL. Set on Railway to the
# Postgres plugin's connection string. Production refuses to boot without it.
DATABASE_URL=

# Session signing. CHANGE THIS to a long random string before any real use.
JWT_SECRET=dev-only-change-me
# Extra secret mixed into OTP hashing. CHANGE before real use.
OTP_PEPPER=dev-only-change-me-too

# Where the frontend runs (for CORS)
WEB_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000

# --- Email ---
# Resend if RESEND_API_KEY is set, else codes print to the console (local dev).
# EMAIL_FROM must be on a domain verified in Resend (e.g. login@yourdomain.com),
# or onboarding@resend.dev for testing (only sends to your own account email).
# A Gmail address is NOT allowed as the sender.
RESEND_API_KEY=
EMAIL_FROM=login@rozipay.xyz
EMAIL_FROM_NAME=RoziPay

# --- BIMI (brand logo in the recipient's inbox, e.g. Gmail/Yahoo) ---
# Not an env var — a DNS + Resend setup checklist, since our emails currently
# show a generic gray icon instead of the RoziPay mark. Do this AFTER
# RESEND_API_KEY is set and rozipay.xyz is verified as a sending domain:
#   1. In Resend, add + verify rozipay.xyz as a domain. This gives you SPF and
#      DKIM DNS records (TXT/CNAME) to add in GoDaddy — add them there.
#   2. Let real mail flow for a while so SPF/DKIM are confirmed passing, THEN
#      add a DMARC TXT record at `_dmarc.rozipay.xyz`, e.g.:
#        v=DMARC1; p=quarantine; rua=mailto:dmarc@rozipay.xyz
#      Do NOT jump straight to p=reject — start at p=quarantine and confirm
#      nothing legitimate breaks first. BIMI requires p=quarantine or p=reject
#      (p=none does not qualify).
#   3. The logo file is ready: web/public/brand/bimi-logo.svg (square SVG
#      Tiny-PS profile, wraps the existing brand mark). Once deployed it's at
#      https://rozipay.xyz/brand/bimi-logo.svg — run it through a BIMI
#      validator (e.g. bimigroup.org/bimi-generator) to confirm it passes.
#   4. Add a BIMI TXT record at `default._bimi.rozipay.xyz`:
#        v=BIMI1; l=https://rozipay.xyz/brand/bimi-logo.svg
#   5. Gmail specifically will NOT show the logo without a paid Verified Mark
#      Certificate (VMC, ~$1300-2500/yr from Entrust/DigiCert) on top of all
#      of the above — Yahoo/AOL and similar show it without one. See
#      CLAUDE.md's BIMI entry for the founder's decision on this (no VMC for
#      now).

# --- Ad-network postback secrets ---
# One shared secret per network for verifying inbound S2S postbacks (HMAC).
# Empty locally falls back to a known dev secret so the demo adapters verify.
# Set real per-network secrets in production.
POSTBACK_SECRET_OFFERHUB=
POSTBACK_SECRET_TAPVID=
POSTBACK_SECRET_SURVEYX=
# tapvid also gates with a static shared token in addition to the signature.
POSTBACK_TOKEN_TAPVID=

# --- CPX Research (REAL, live survey network) ---
# POSTBACK_SECRET_CPX is the "app secure hash" from the CPX dashboard. It signs
# BOTH the inbound postback (md5(`${trans_id}-${secret}`)) and the per-user
# survey-wall URL. Production refuses to boot while it is still the dev default.
# NEVER commit the real value — this repo is public.
POSTBACK_SECRET_CPX=
# Public app id (safe in the browser — it appears in the survey-wall URL).
CPX_APP_ID=34405
# Sanity cap: refuse to credit a single survey worth more than this many points
# (600 points = $1 of our revenue), bounding the damage if the secret ever leaks.
CPX_MAX_POINTS=20000
# Pin postbacks to CPX's published IPs (188.40.3.73, 157.90.97.92,
# 2a01:4f8:d0a:30ff::2). Leave false until you've confirmed the IP the app
# actually observes behind Railway's proxy — otherwise you'd silently reject real
# (already paid-for) completions.
CPX_ENFORCE_IP=false

# --- Staff paging over Telegram (alerts.ts) ---
# Pages staff over Telegram the instant a HIGH-severity fraud/reconciliation
# flag is first raised (fraud.ts's flagOnce). No token => a quiet no-op.
# Reuses the same bot as the Telegram login/mini-app feature (set that token on
# Railway; nothing further is needed there for this).
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=

# 2026-09-05: alerts now go to NAMED INDIVIDUALS by Telegram DM, picked in
# /staff -> Feature flags -> Staff alerts, not a shared group. That screen can
# only add someone who has already started the bot (Telegram has no "look up
# by username" API — TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET below is what makes that
# knowable at all). Set any long random string; the webhook self-registers at
# boot once both this and TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN are set. Empty => the webhook is
# never registered and nobody can be added as a DM recipient.
TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Where Telegram POSTs updates. Defaults to the live API domain in config.ts —
# only set this to override for another environment.
TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_URL=

# Legacy fallback, kept so nothing breaks mid-migration off the old design: a
# shared Telegram GROUP chat id. Once every real recipient is added as a named
# DM above, clear this — a group risks being (or becoming) more public than a
# fraud/reconciliation alert should ever reach.
# To get a chat id: create a Telegram group, add your bot to it, send any
# message in the group, then open
#   https://api.telegram.org/bot<TOKEN>/getUpdates
# in a browser and read the negative "chat":{"id": ...} number back out (group
# chat ids are negative). Test it live from /staff -> Feature flags -> Staff
# alerts (the "Send test alert" button, POST /staff/alerts/test).
TELEGRAM_ALERT_CHAT_ID=

# --- Per-user deposit addresses (docs/CUSTODY_SPEC.md § 5, step 1) ---
# The ACCOUNT-LEVEL xpub for BEP20 (m/44'/60'/0'), derived OFFLINE from a seed
# phrase that must never appear in this file, this repo, or any Railway
# variable other than this one. Empty => feature off; every user shares the
# one treasury address exactly as before. This value alone can derive an
# unlimited number of user addresses but can NEVER sign a transaction — no
# private key is ever present in this codebase.
CUSTODY_XPUB_BEP20=

# --- BscScan API key (on-demand BNB history only) ---
# Used by GET /wallet/bnb/history (api/src/bscscan.ts) via Etherscan's V2
# multichain API (chainid 56). NOTE: as of 2025 the free Etherscan/BscScan tier
# does NOT cover BSC address history — it needs a paid plan. Empty (or a
# free-tier key) means the BNB screen falls back to the native scanner below +
# our own withdrawal rows. Key at https://etherscan.io/apis
BSCSCAN_API_KEY=

# --- Native (BNB) deposit scanner ---
# Walks full block bodies to record incoming BNB into deposit addresses
# (history rows + a "BNB received" push). OFF by default — one
# eth_getBlockByNumber(_, true) per block, 24/7, cost driven by the chain's
# block rate not user activity. Turn on only behind an RPC provider whose free
# allowance sustains it (RPC_BEP20 currently leads with NodeReal).
NATIVE_DEPOSIT_SCAN_ENABLED=false
# Blocks covered per tick. Post-Maxwell BSC (~200k blocks/day) needs ≳400 at
# the default interval just to keep pace; 600 gives margin. Each unit is one
# more full-block RPC call per tick — watch the provider dashboard.
NATIVE_DEPOSIT_SCAN_BLOCK_RANGE=100

# ============================================================================
# SPEND CEILINGS ON PAID EXTERNAL CALLS  (api/src/costGuard.ts)
# ============================================================================
# These are NOT throughput tuning. They are the ceiling that holds when a
# specific safeguard turns out to have a gap, which has happened twice on this
# project (the BNB native scanner walking every block, then the USDT scanner's
# steady-state poll — both found by looking at a bill). Set PER REPLICA: the
# counter lives in the process, so N replicas means N budgets.
#
# Steady state at launch is roughly 80-100 RPC calls an hour. The default is
# ~50x that, so it never shapes normal traffic and still stops an unattended
# loop at a known number. Low-priority work (scanners, screen reads, the
# hourly reconciliation) is cut off at 80% of the limit; the rest is reserved
# for calls that are part of moving someone's money right now.
# 0 disables the ceiling entirely.
RPC_MAX_CALLS_PER_HOUR=5000
# Block-explorer reads (bscscan.ts). A DAY window, matching the shape of the
# free-tier allowance it actually runs out against.
EXPLORER_MAX_CALLS_PER_DAY=20000

# How long a user's on-chain BNB (gas) balance is reused before asking the
# chain again. This read is PER USER, so unlike the fixed-cadence scanners its
# cost grows with the user base. Raising it trades freshness for a directly
# proportional cut in paid calls. (The withdraw path never trusts this cache
# for a decision it has not just re-read.)
GAS_BALANCE_CACHE_MS=60000

# How often the treasury-vs-ledger reconciliation runs. One multicall per 300
# deposit addresses, so this one also grows with the user base. Slow it here
# rather than in a deploy.
RECONCILE_INTERVAL_MS=3600000

# ============================================================================
# DATABASE TLS  (audit finding A-05)
# ============================================================================
# Best answer: point DATABASE_URL at Railway's PRIVATE network
# (*.railway.internal). That traffic never leaves the project, needs no TLS,
# and nothing below applies.
#
# Over a PUBLIC endpoint the server certificate must be verified, or the
# connection is encrypted against everyone except whoever is actually in the
# middle. Supply the CA and verification switches on automatically:
#   DATABASE_CA_CERT       the PEM itself (paste it, newlines and all), or
#   DATABASE_CA_CERT_PATH  a path to a .pem file
#   DATABASE_TLS_SERVERNAME  set this when the certificate names a different
#                            host than the connection string does — that exact
#                            mismatch is why verification was off in the first
#                            place, and naming the right host fixes it properly
#                            instead of giving up on checking.
# With none of these set, the API still connects but warns loudly on every boot
# that the server is unauthenticated.
DATABASE_CA_CERT=
DATABASE_CA_CERT_PATH=
DATABASE_TLS_SERVERNAME=

# ============================================================================
# WHICH PROXIES req.ip MAY BELIEVE  (audit finding A-04)
# ============================================================================
# req.ip feeds the per-IP rate limits, the IP fraud rules (ip_reuse,
# referral-ring-by-IP) and the postback IP pin. Behind a platform edge, the
# socket peer is the EDGE, identical for every user — so the real client has to
# come from X-Forwarded-For, and only from proxies we actually trust.
#
# ⚠️ THIS REPLACED TRUST_PROXY_HOPS, WHICH NO LONGER WORKS. Fastify 5.12.1
# neutered numeric trustProxy as its fix for GHSA-3m5p-2c4r-xxw2: a hop count
# now silently resolves req.ip to the edge's own address and ignores
# X-Forwarded-For. Silently — nothing errors, per-IP limits just collapse into
# one global bucket. If TRUST_PROXY is set to a number the API ignores it and
# warns.
#
# Default (leave unset): loopback, linklocal, uniquelocal, 100.64.0.0/10
# — loopback and private space, plus carrier-grade NAT, which `uniquelocal`
# does not cover and several hosts use internally.
#
# ⚠️ NEVER set this to `true`: that trusts the LEFT-most X-Forwarded-For entry,
# which is the one a client writes, so any user could claim any IP.
#
# If the edge in front of this API has a PUBLIC address, none of the defaults
# match it and req.ip becomes that edge address for everyone. The API logs a
# warning naming the peer when it detects exactly that; add the network shown.
TRUST_PROXY=
